meta {
  name: Upsert Zip Markets
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/platform/mailer-zip-markets
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{platformAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "entries": [
      { "zip5": "99998", "market": "Bruno Test Market" }
    ]
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("creates on the first run and updates on every one after", function () {
    // The collection is re-runnable, so exactly one of these is true each time
    // — which is the whole point of an upsert keyed on (agencyId, zip5).
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.upserted + body.updated).to.be.at.least(0);
    expect(body).to.have.property("upserted");
    expect(body).to.have.property("updated");
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT /platform/mailer-zip-markets — create or correct ZIP → market rows

  The table that replaces ApexReports' Google Sheet (`1EubGmhG4s…`). The market
  decides the local-presence phone number printed on the mail piece, so an
  unmapped ZIP is not cosmetic — it mails the default market's number into a
  town that number is not local to.

  - **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`)
  - **Permission:** `platform:mailers:write`
  - **Data scope:** none (`@SkipTenant()`, `@SkipBranch()`)

  ## Why `PUT` rather than `POST`

  Every write is an upsert keyed on `(agencyId, zip5)`, so the operation is
  idempotent: sending the same block twice leaves the table in the same state. A
  `POST` would imply a second row per send, which the unique index refuses.

  ## Body
  | field | rules |
  |---|---|
  | entries | 1–1000 rows. The seeded table is 564, so a paste of the whole thing fits while a runaway client cannot turn one request into an unbounded `bulkWrite`. |
  | entries[].zip5 | **Exactly five digits**, never zero-padded to fit. |
  | entries[].market | 1–60 chars, free text (`Tulsa`, `Oklahoma City`, `580 Group`). |
  | entries[].agencyId | Optional. Omit for the platform-global row — nothing writes a non-null value in this ticket. |

  ⚠ Five digits with no padding is deliberate. The seed source carries a
  four-digit typo (`4031`, which should be `74031`); stored, it would be a row
  that can never match an address and still looks like a mapping, and a
  "helpful" zero-pad would map `04031` — Freeport, Maine — to Tulsa.

  ## Rows this endpoint does not overwrite

  None: `PUT` is the correction path and `$set`s the market. The **seed** is the
  one that holds back, writing with `$setOnInsert` so a correction made here is
  never silently reverted by the next deploy. `source` records who decided —
  `seed`, `preview` (an operator resolving an unmatched ZIP mid-run) or `manual`.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  { "upserted": 1, "updated": 0 }
  ```

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — a ZIP that is not five digits, an empty market, an empty
    `entries` array, or more than 1000 rows.
  - `401` / `403` — missing token, or missing `platform:mailers:write`.
}
